MonteleCarlo sampling is a technique in simulation, which is used to generate a random number from a distribution.
This is done by generating a number of samples (called "burn-in") from the distribution and then generating random numbers from this distribution by sampling from them. The distribution may be discrete or continuous. We will use a discrete distribution in this tutorial. An example would be sampling 1,000 integers from 1 to 90000 in such a way that each integer is equally likely. This is called "Uniform sampling".
The output of this process is the random sample. In this case, a single sample of a 1 is generated. It is assumed that the sampling distribution is known. That is, the output from Monte Carlo simulation is not random. There is one "true" random value out of all the generated values. If the true value of the sample is very close to the sampled value, then the Monte-Caralo sampling technique is said to have generated a "close-to-random" sample (although it is unlikely that it will happen with any given distribution).
Here is an example of random sampling with a uniform distribution:
// Generate a set of 10 random integers int x = {0, 0, 1};
